are limited in performance and consequently mostly used to enter short texts, such as passwords or names. Further, they require some degree of training due to their unfamiliarity to some users, compared to standard desktop and touchscreen keyboards that users are already familiar and proﬁcient with.
Hence, recently, several groups investigated the feasibility of utilizing standard keyboards for text entry in immersive HMD-based VR focusing on different hand representations, including no hand representations [29], minimalistic ﬁngertip rendering [7, 8], video see-through [2, 19] to full inverse kinematic hand models [?, 17]. In 2017, Logitech made a toolkit available which utilizes a blended camera view [2, 28]. However, apart from using no hand model (which results in unacceptable high error rates without auto-decoders [29]), all proposed techniques rely on the availability of external tracking systems integrated into the environment (e.g., using infrared-based outside-in tracking systems) or in the HMD (e.g., using cameras). As cameras or external hand tracking systems are not widely integrated into the majority of HMDs, this can potentially hinder the widespread use of text entry on physical keyboards.
Our work extends these previous works by investigating if efﬁcient text entry in VR can be achieved through an alternative sensing modality. Speciﬁcally, we look at touch-sensitive keyboards through capacitive sensing, which allow to visualize a ﬁngertip on a physical key before a character has been entered. In fact, there is a large body of work on touch-sensitive keyboards using capacitive sensing that has been investigated outside of VR (e.g, [1, 10, 22, 25, 26]). It has been demonstrated that touch-sensitive keyboards have the potential to be commercialized (e.g., [4]).
In this paper, we focus on utilizing touch-sensitive physical keyboards for efﬁcient text entry in VR as a tool for enabling portable virtual work environments [6]. We envision that future ofﬁce workers only need access to a laptop with integrated touch-sensing keyboard and trackpad as well as a untethered VR Head-Mounted Display (HMD) for working productively on the go. Speciﬁcally, we do not assume any tracking capability of the mobile HMD beyond 3 degrees of freedom rotation sensing. As a ﬁrst step towards realizing this vision it is crucial to understand if future mass-manufactured touch-sensitive physical keyboards lend themselves for efﬁcient text entry in VR.

