We are focusing on physical keyboards as research has shown that typing on those physical keyboards substantially outperforms text entry on multi-touch tablets, which could be considered an alternative text entry method [8].
In this work, we built a physical keyboard with capacitive sensing, which allows to sense and visualize the user’s ﬁnger tip before a key has been pressed. This could be beneﬁcial for virtual ofﬁce work on the go.
2 RELATED WORK
Our work draws on the domain of text entry in VR, speciﬁcally typing on keyboards, as well on the domain of touch-sensitive physical keyboards.
2.1 Text Entry in VR
Text entry has been extensively researched (see [18, 31] for surveys and overviews). However, relatively few text entry methods have been proposed for VR.
Prior work has investigated a variety of wearable gloves, where touching between the hand ﬁngers may represent different characters or words, e.g., [3, 13, 30]. Such devices enable a mobile, eyes-free text entry. However, most of them require a considerable learning effort, and may limit the user ability to use other input devices while interacting in VR, such as game controllers.
The mainstream mobile phone touchscreen keyboard might be a good text entry option [5]. Recent work has investigated the feasibility of using multi-touch screens for text entry in VR, e.g., using a smartphone [16], a face-mounted display [9] or a multi-touch tablet [8]. Speciﬁcally, Grubert et al. [8] indicated a relatively low text entry performance using multi-touch tablets, Guggenheimer et al. [9] and Kim et al. [16] did not report standard text entry metrics.
While we do not investigate this small type of touchscreen keyboard in this paper, we do believe it has potential.
Recent research has investigated the feasibility of typing on a physical full-sized keyboard in VR.
An obvious problem is the lack of visual feedback. Without visual feedback users’ typing performance degraded substantially. However, by blending video of the user’s hands into VR the adverse performance differential signiﬁcantly reduced [19].
Fundamentally, there are three solution strategies for supporting keyboards in VR. First, by providing complete visual feedback by blending the user’s hands into VR. Second, by decoding (autocorrecting) the user’s typing to compensate for noise induced by the lack of feedback. Third, by investigating hybrid approaches, such as minimal visual feedback, which may or may not require a decoder to compensate for any noise induced by the method.
Walker et al. [29] investigated typing on a physical keyboard with no visual feedback. They found that the character error rate (CER) was unacceptably high but could be reduced to an average 3.5% CER using an auto-correcting decoder.
McGill et al. [19] investigated typing on a physical keyboard in Augmented Virtuality [20] with camera view on a physical keyboard.
The closest work to ours is from Grubert et al. [7, 8]. In 2018, they investigated the performance of physical and touch keyboards and physical/virtual co-location for VR text entry. They proposed to use minimalistic ﬁngertip rendering as hand representation and indicated that this representation is as efﬁcient as a video-see through of the user’s physical hands [7]. Subsequently, similar studies have investigated further hand representations, such as semi-transparent hand models [17]. Besides, optical outside-in tracking systems with sub-millimiter accuracy such as Optitrack Prime series (e.g., in [7,8,17]), also commodity tracking devices such as the Leap Motion have been utilized [12] for hand tracking on physical keyboards.
Our work extends these previous works by investigating if efﬁcient text entry in VR can be achieved through an alternative sensing modality. Speciﬁcally, we look at touch-sensitive keyboards through

capacitive sensing, which allow to visualize a ﬁngertip on a physical key before a character has been entered.
2.2 Touch-sensitive physical keyboards
There have been several approaches in extending the basic input capabilities of physical keyboard beyond individual button presses. Speciﬁcally, embedding capacitive sensing into keyboards has been studied by various researchers. It lends itself to detect ﬁnger events on and slightly above keys and can be integrated into massmanufacturing processes. Habib et al. [10] and Tung et al. [26] proposed to use capacitive sensing embedded into a full physical keyboard to allow touchpad operation on the keyboard surface. Shi et al. developed microgestures on capacitive sensing keys [24, 25].
Extending the idea of LCD-programmable keyboards [14], Block et al. extended the output capabilities of touch-sensitive, capacitive sensing keyboard by using a top-mounted projector [1].
Our work utilizes capacitive sensing for detecting resting ﬁngers on individual keys, which notiﬁes a user of the selected key before a character is entered through a key press.
