4.1 Procedure and Task
After welcoming, participants were asked to ﬁll out a demographic questionnaire. Thereafter, we conducted a calibration phase, in which participants entered text for 5 minutes for proﬁling reasons and in which retro-reﬂective markers where attached to the ﬁngernails.
The order of the conditions was balanced across participants. In either condition, participants were shown a series of stimulus sentences. For an individual stimulus sentence, participants were asked to type it as quickly and as accurately as possible. Participants typed stimulus sentences for 15 minutes in each condition. Participants were allowed to use the backspace key to correct errors.
The conditions were separated by a 5-minute break, in which participants ﬁlled out a the SSQ simulator questionnaire [15], the NASA TLX questionnaire [11], the IPQ [21] spatial presence questionnaire and the Flow-Short-Scale [23]. After the ﬁnal condition, participants were asked to ﬁll out a self-assessment questionnaire, which was followed by a semi-structured interview. The experiment was carried out in a 90-minute session.
Stimulus sentences were drawn from the mobile email phrase set [27], which provides a set of text entry stimulus sentences that have been veriﬁed to be both externally valid and easy to memorize for participants. Participants were shown stimulus phrases randomly drawn from the set.
An OptiTrack Prime 13 outside-in tracking system was used for spatial tracking of ﬁnger tips, the HMD, an external monitor and the keyboard. The monitor and keyboard were tracked for logging reasons. An Oculus Rift CV1 was used as HMD. The physical keyboard described in section 2.2 was used. The virtual environment was showing the virtual keyboard and a monitor resting on a desk. In line with previous work [7, 8], the phrases and the entered text were visualized both on the monitor and directly above the keyboard.

4.2 Results The performance results are summarized in Figure 3.
Figure 3: Left: Task completion time in word-per-minute. Right: Character Error rate. FT: FINGERTRACKING, CT: CAPACITIVETOUCH
Statistical signiﬁcance tests for entry rate and error rate were carried out using paired, two-tailed t-tests with a signiﬁcance level of α = 0.05. The analysis where checked for appropriateness against the dataset, log transformed data was used for error rate. Subjective feedback was analyzed with non-parametric Wilcoxon signed-rank tests.
Entry rate was measured in words-per-minute (wpm), with a word deﬁned as ﬁve consecutive characters, including spaces.The entry rate was 33.2 wpm (sd = 20.3) for FINGERTRACKING and 31.5 wpm (sd = 17.0) for CAPACITIVETOUCH. The difference in entry rate was not signiﬁcant (t(5) = 1.18, p = 0.293).
Error rate was measured as character error rate (CER). CER is the minimum number of character-level insertion, deletion and substitution operations required to transform the response text into the stimulus text, divided by the number of characters in the stimulus text. The character error rate (CER) was 1.1% (sd = 0.07) for FINGERTRACKING and 1.5% (sd = 0.1) for CAPACITIVETOUCH. The difference in error rate was not signiﬁcant (t(5) = −0.31, p = 0.76). No signiﬁcant differences could be indicated for SSQ, NASA TLX, IPQ or the Flow-Short-Scale.
5 DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION Touch-sensitive physical keyboards allow for on-surface interaction, with sensing integrated into the keyboard itself.

