Our study indicates that writing on a touch-sensitive keyboard has the potential to be as efﬁcient as typing using ﬁngertip visualization, and, hence, external tracking or camera systems might not be needed to support text entry in VR, which could be beneﬁcial for virtual ofﬁce work on the go.
However, the small number of participants do not allow to easily generalize those ﬁndings to the population. Also, it is unclear how typing proﬁciency could inﬂuence typing performance on a touchsensitive keyboard. Please note, that our keyboard was not using the standard 104-key layout due to lack of availability of compatible PCBs. Typing on this smaller keyboard could result in absolute performance differences compared to a larger keyboard, but for both conditions. Hence, the relative performance differences (or the lack thereof) can be seen as transferable to other keyboards.
Our work is a ﬁrst step in making touch-sensitive keyboards available for VR text entry. If touch-sensitive keyboards will become commercially widely available they could become an efﬁcient tool for VR text entry. In future work, we want to thoroughly investigate the performance envelope of touch-sensitive physical keyboards.
